sentences.t:
El bueno de mio Çid non lo tardó por nada:
“¿Do sodes, caboso? venid acá, Minaya;
de lo que a vos ca[d]ió vos non gradeçedes nada;

sentences.l:
The good of my Çid not it delayed for nothing:
“Where are-you, accomplished-one? come here, Minaya;
of that which to you fe[l]l you not give-thanks nothing;

sentences.t:
E cras ha la mañana ir vos hedes sin falla
con cavallos desta quinta que yo he ganada,
con siellas e con frenos e con señas espadas;

A ojo lo[s] avien los del que en buen ora nasco,
cuédanse que es almofalla, ca non vienen con mandado;
el rey don Alfonso seíse santiguando.

sentences.l:
In eye the[m] they-had those of-him who in good hour was-born,
they-think that it-is an-army, and not they-come with message;
the king don Alfonso was-himself crossing.

sentences.t:
Dixo rey don Alfons: “Reçíbolos de grado.
Gradéscolo a mio Çid que tal don me ha enbiado;
aun vea ora que de mí sea pagado.”

sentences.l:
Said king don Alfons: “I-receive-them gladly.
I-thank-it to my Çid that such gift to-me has sent;
yet may-I-see hour that with me he-may-be pleased.”

for so easily to-conquer kings of-the field,
as if them he-found dead to-bring-for-himself the horses
for this that he does we will-have trouble.”

sentences.t:
“Las nuevas del Çid mucho van adelant,
demandemos sus fijas pora con ellas casar;
creçremos en nuestra ondra e iremos adelant.”

sentences.l:
“The news of-the Çid much go forward,
let-us-ask-for his daughters for with them to-marry;
we-will-grow in our honour and we-will-go forward.”
